adr: 0360
file: lib/dialect/pastoml.pas
kills: lib_toml_errors
why: Descend stops refusing a header that passes *through* a table a dotted key created, so `a.b.c = 1` followed by `[a.b.d]` is accepted. It is the mirror image of the rule ParseHeader applies to the last segment, and the two are separate arms: this mutation SURVIVED on the day it was written, because every refusal in the case landed on a last segment and nothing reached this one. That is TOML's one-definition rule read the lenient way, and it is the half of the format a parser is likeliest to get wrong -- the rules turn on how a table came to exist and not on whether it exists.
--- old
      else if forHeader and got^.dotted then begin
        e := errSyntax;
        cur := nil
      end
--- new
      else if forHeader and got^.dotted then
        cur := got
